	(descr "TE Connectivity / Schrack Power PCB Relay RT1, part RT314012 (1 form C, 16A/250VAC, 12VDC coil). Datasheet ENG_DS_RT1_0718, re-fetched and pdftotext-extracted directly this session from https://www.te.com/commerce/DocumentDelivery/DDEController?Action=showdoc&DocId=Data+Sheet%7FRT1%7F0718%7Fpdf%7FEnglish%7FENG_DS_RT1_0718.pdf%7F9-1393239-8 (footer edition date 06/25). Replaces Omron G5LE-1 (Relay_SPDT) on K2/K3 -- the G5LE-1's COM pin sits only 3.559mm (as-placed) / ~3.50mm (nominal) from a coil pin on every rotation, failing both the 8.0mm reinforced-creepage bar and the 6.0mm pollution-degree-independent clearance minimum; no footprint or rotation change can fix a fixed terminal topology. INSULATION DATA table (p.4, independently re-extracted): 'Clearance/creepage between contact and coil >=10/10mm', 'Initial dielectric strength between contact and coil 5000Vrms', 'Material group of insulation parts IIIa', matching the Features line 'Product PCB RELAY RT1 -- 5kV/10mm coil-contact, reinforced insulation'. COIL VERSIONS DC COIL table (p.3): code 012 = 12VDC rated, 8.4V operate, 1.2V release, 360ohm+/-10%, 400mW -- identical R/P to the outgoing G5LE-1's own declared coil (elec/src/modules.ato), so the existing 100ohm/15V dropper is unaffected. CONTACT DATA table (p.2): RT314 (16A pinning) rated 16A/250VAC, 400VAC max switching voltage, AgNi 90/10 contacts. PRODUCT INFORMATION table (p.7): RT314012 row, Austria PN 9-1393239-5 / China PN 1-1649328-3. PCB LAYOUT/TERMINAL ASSIGNMENT (p.5, '16A, pinning 5mm' column, 'Bottom view on solder pins'): dimensioned drawing gives 8 holes (Ø1.3mm+0.1/-0) in a 2-row x 4-column grid, 7.5mm+/-0.2 row pitch; the contact side's 3 columns span 20.3mm+/-0.2 total (5.04mm+/-0.15 sub-pitch each side of the middle column), overall pad-field width 22.65mm+/-0.2; THT body envelope 29.0mm(W) x 12.7mm(D) x 15.7mm(H), 3.6mm+/-0.2 lead length below the seating plane. The same page's schematic labels the 3 contact terminals '12 11 14' in that same left-to-right (near-coil-to-far-coil) order for the '1 form C (CO) contact' diagram; cross-checked against the sibling 'Relay_SPST_Schrack-RT1-16A-FormA_RM5mm.kicad_mod' stock footprint (Form A = NO-only, no NC contact), which carries only pads '11'/'14' and omits '12' entirely -- confirming the manufacturer's own terminal convention is 11=COM (pole), 12=NC, 14=NO, A1/A2=coil (non-polarized DC coil). Pad geometry (position/size/drill/shape/layers for all 8 physical pad instances) copied verbatim from KiCad 10's stock 'Relay_THT.pretty/Relay_SPDT_Schrack-RT1-16A-FormC_RM5mm.kicad_mod' (itself already datasheet-sourced and pad-for-pad consistent with the above dimensioned drawing, independently confirmed here) -- NOT re-derived from scratch, since the stock file already exactly reproduces the datasheet's own dimensions. RENUMBERED relative to the stock file and to the manufacturer's own '11/12/14/A1/A2' terminal labels: this project's existing 'Relay_SPDT' atopile component (elec/src/components.ato) declares a fixed pin convention -- 1=COM, 2=coil1, 3=NO, 4=NC, 5=coil2 -- established for the outgoing G5LE-1 (whose stock footprint pads are literally named '1'..'5') and already relied on by elec/domain_manifest.yaml's discharge.k_dis1/discharge.k_dis2 isolator declarations (groups coil:['2','5'], contacts:['1','3','4']), so this renumbering requires zero change to that manifest. It also avoids a real hazard: gen_pcb_skeleton.py/resync_pcb_netlist.py fall back to POSITIONAL pad-index matching whenever a footprint's pad numbers don't exact-match a netlist pin string, and that positional index is computed over ALL physical pad entries including this part's genuine manufacturer-duplicated contact pads (11/12/14 each have two physical solder holes, one per row, for 16A current sharing -- confirmed against the datasheet's own 8-hole dimensioned drawing, not a stock-footprint error) -- with 8 physical pads but only 5 netlist-declared pins, naive positional fallback would cross-assign a coil net onto one of a contact pin's two physical holes. Renaming pad numbers here to exact-match the netlist's '1'..'5' strings makes every pad resolve via the exact-match path instead (which correctly gives BOTH physical holes of a renamed pad the same net, since the match is re-derived per pad instance from the same net lookup), sidestepping the positional-fallback path entirely. Pad local rotation flattened from the stock file's 180 degrees to 0 -- verified electrically/dimensionally identical for these axis-symmetric rect/oval pads (a 180-degree rotation of a rectangle or oval about its own centre is congruent to the unrotated shape), so this does not change any pad's effective copper geometry or the achievable-gap figure below. Courtyard (29.9 x 13.6mm, -2.8,-10.55 to 27.1,3.05) and silkscreen/fab graphics also copied verbatim from the stock file. Achievable coil<->nearest-contact separation, recomputed independently from this file's own pad table: nearest pair is pad 2 (coil1, local x=0, width 3mm) to pad 4 (NC, local x=15.26, width 2mm): 15.26 - 1.5 - 1.0 = 12.760mm edge-to-edge -- clears both the 8.0mm reinforced-creepage bar and the 6.0mm clearance minimum with margin, and clears the manufacturer's own >=10/10mm coil-to-contact rating independently. No 3D model reference carried over (the stock file's own model points at a sibling RT2/DPDT step file, unverified for this SPDT part -- omitted rather than propagated).")
